The proof of the main theorem follows the general strategy of Gogic--Tomasevic, Theorem~1.1 from \cite[Theorem~1.1]{GogicTomasevic-AM}. The classification relies on the fact that maps satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{eq:a certain product}
			\phi(A^k \circ B) = \phi(A)^k \circ \phi(B), \qquad \text{for all } A,B \in M_n(\F),
		\end{equation}
preserve $(k+1)$-potents along with their ranks and natural partial order, and that they are orthoadditive on these elements. A notable difference from the case $k=1$ is that if $\operatorname{char}(\F)$ is positive and divides $k$, $(k+1)$-potents may fail to be diagonalizable, which requires more refined arguments in order to handle the mixed Jordan--power preservers effectively.

